 <section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The market</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Carolina Forest home prices in 2026</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">The two best reads, each with its own lens: Redfin's Carolina Forest page showed a median sale price of $418,000, up 7.7 percent year over year, with homes going under contract in about 105 days as of mid-2026. SC Realtors data for ZIP 29579 showed a $464,710 single-family median in December 2025, up from $426,500 a year earlier, on 129 days of market time. Condos along the corridor, the area's entry tier, ran a median near $215,500, up about 17 percent.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px">Context makes those numbers make sense: the Horry County median sits near $329,000 and the City of Myrtle Beach near $266,000, but both are dragged down by condo-heavy stock. Carolina Forest trades at a premium to the county because it is almost entirely single-family product in amenity communities, and local press has described it as a seller's market even while beach condos favor buyers. Trailing twelve months, roughly 1,330 homes changed hands here. Current numbers for any specific community: ask, and see the <a href="/market-reports/july-2026/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">July 2026 market report</a> for the region-wide picture.</p></div></section>
 
